				\begin{document}$$h({\bf{s}})=[{\bf{Ws}}\ge 0],\,\mathrm{where}\,\,{\bf{W}}\in {{\mathbb{R}}}^{m,n}\,\,\mathrm{and}\,\,{{\bf{W}}}_{ij} \sim {\mathcal{N}}(0,1),$$\end{document}h(s)=[Ws≥0],whereW∈Rm,nandWij~N(0,1),where [ ⋅ ] indicates an element-wise Iverson bracket, meaning that [xi] = 1 if xi is true and 0 otherwise. Essentially, each element of the Ws product is a dot product of s and a random n-dimensional hyperplane. Each of the m hyperplanes splits the n-dimensional space into two complementary subspaces, thereby determining the subspace to which s belongs, based on the sign of each dot product. These signs represent the bits of the resulting m-dimensional hash. Given that every hyperplane intersects the origin, the likelihood of two binned spectra si and sj sharing the same hash is a function of their cosine similarity80:1\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
